text:
{
  "errors": [
    {
      "span": "Om. The Dharma, characterized by qualities such as invisibility, is declared. Om. 1/2/21.",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "The qualities such as invisibility are declared to be those of Vishnu.",
      "category": "addition",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "“That which is invisible and ungraspable,”",
      "category": "translation",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "“Just as the web is spun and gathered by the spider, just as plants arise from the earth, just as hairs and body hair arise from a living being, so does the universe arise here from the Imperishable,”",
      "category": "omission",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "it is inferred that the Supreme (Para) is distinct from the Imperishable (Akshara).",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "Therefore, it was declared: “The Dharma, characterized by qualities such as invisibility, is declared.”",
      "category": "addition",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "After citing the analogy of the earth and stating, “The universe arises here from the Imperishable,”",
      "category": "omission",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "the subsequent reference to “That which is beyond the Imperishable” (tat-paratara) leads to the understanding of Prakriti, as per the memory (smriti) that “The Unchanging (Kutastha) is called the Imperishable (Akshara).”",
      "category": "inaccuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "Furthermore, Hiranyagarbha is understood from the term “Brahman” and the reference to “That which is beyond the Imperishable.”",
      "category": "omission",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "“One who knows Him thus becomes immortal here,”",
      "category": "translation",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "“That action is the delight of Hari; that is the knowledge, that is the thought by which...,”",
      "category": "omission",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "“Now, two kinds of knowledge are to be known: the higher (para) and the lower (apara). That which consists of the Vedas, their limbs, sub-limbs, and inner limbs is the lower. But the higher is that by which Hari is to be known, who is that invisible, attributeless, Supreme Self beyond the Supreme,”",
      "category": "inaccuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "the subject matter of the higher knowledge (para-vidya), whose nature is Dharma, is understood to be Vishnu alone, characterized by qualities such as invisibility.",
      "category": "addition",
      "severity": "major"
    }
  ]
}
